From d7df09dd90cac7f88c26e2f4129f279b2ad7759a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:58:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: a film in the fields of interlaced video is put
 back together and shown at its pace

A stream can now say that it is a film in its fields (CDemuxStreamVideo::filmCadence, carried by
CDVDStreamInfo with the rate of its coded frames): its rate is then the film's, it is not
interlaced, and it is progressive for the choice of the display mode. For such a stream:

- CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg sets up, for the whole stream and whatever each frame says, the graph of
  its cadence (FieldCadence.h, GraphOf()): fieldmatch weaves each frame with the fields that make
  a whole picture, bwdif deinterlaces only a frame that no weave makes whole (a cut, a stretch of
  video), and decimate leaves out the picture that repeats (one in five in 3:2, one in six for 25
  in 30; always the one that differs least: by default it took the first picture after the graph
  was set up for a scene change and left it out). The buffer source is given the rate of the coded
  frames, which decimate needs. What comes out is marked DVP_FLAG_FILM. Not where the deinterlace
  method of the video is Off. The player information shows the method ("film 3:2 (fieldmatch,
  decimate)"); kodi.log says when the graph is set up, where the film's cadence stops and comes
  back (CCadenceWatch, the first 20 times), and when it closes how many frames went in and pictures
  came out. Such a stream is decoded by FFmpeg on the processor (CODEC_FORCE_SOFTWARE), where the
  filters have the fields.
- CVideoPlayerVideo shows each picture so marked once, at the pace of the film (CFilmTimer, as for
  soft telecine). Its pictures come out of the graph with the time of a coded frame they were taken
  from, up to a coded frame off the pace, so the times start again from the stream only where they
  are off by more than a picture of the film (SetFilmPace()); a picture not put back together goes
  as coded. The rate that the renderer is given is the film's, not doubled for an interlaced frame,
  and the stream stays progressive for the mode: one mode for the whole stream.
- fieldmatch warns of every frame it cannot match: in a log at debug level that was 30 lines a
  second of video. They are left out; CDVDVideoCodecFFmpeg says it instead.

Nothing sets a stream so yet (the next commit does): every stream plays with the same graph, the
same flags and the same times as before.

Measured on the host with the FFmpeg 9.0.2 of the image, on a synthetic corpus whose progressive
source is known byte for byte (3:2, 2:2 in phase and one field off, NTSC 2:2, 25 in 30; fine
moving detail, a slow pan, film grain, a cartoon on twos; MPEG-2 at 6 Mbit/s): every picture that
the graph gives is the film's, field for field (each field one of the decoded copies of that field
of that picture), and every picture of the film is shown once, but the first and the last of a 2:2
one field off, whose other field is not in the stream. Against the source: 36.98 dB where bwdif
gives 35.78 (3:2), 38.33 against 36.68 (2:2), 41.30 against 38.96 (3:2 of a cartoon on twos);
uncompressed, identical to the source.
